            "title": "[FRONTEND] Complete site pages with a shared header/footer and JS-driven dishes",
            "description": "The landing page (index.html) is mostly built, but the rest of the site is not. Right now the header and footer are copy-pasted into every page (index.html, pages/contact.html) and have already started to drift apart — the navbar on index.html uses class \"navbar flex\" while contact.html uses \"navbar\", so editing one page does not update the others. Navigation does not work either: every nav link points to href=\"#\". pages/contact.html is an empty stub with only an <h1>Contact Us</h1>. And the \"Our Best Seller Dishes\" section is hardcoded in HTML, while a half-finished inline <script> at the bottom of index.html dumps a plain text list into #dish-list.\n\nYour job is to finish the site. There are four parts:\n\n1. Build out the remaining pages referenced by the navbar so the site feels complete: pages/menu.html (Menu), pages/foods.html (Foods), pages/about.html (About Us), and finish pages/contact.html with a real contact section (heading, contact form and/or contact details — not just an <h1>).\n\n2. Share ONE header and ONE footer across all pages. Every page — index.html and everything in pages/ — must use the exact same header and footer markup and styling. Do not keep copy-pasting them. Recommended approach: load the header/footer from a single source with JavaScript (e.g. one components.js that builds them and injects into a placeholder on each page), so editing it once updates every page. Acceptable alternative: keep the markup on each page but make it identical and driven by shared CSS. Either way, fix the current drift so they are truly the same everywhere.\n\n3. Make navigation smooth. Replace every href=\"#\" in the navbar (and relevant footer links) with real, working links to the correct pages. Watch the relative paths — index.html is at the project root but the other pages live inside pages/, so links must resolve correctly in both directions (root to pages/, and pages/ to root and page-to-page). Highlight the active page in the nav (e.g. an \"active\" class) so users know where they are. No broken links and no flashes of unstyled content.\n\n4. Inject the dishes list with JavaScript. The dishes on index.html must be rendered from JavaScript, not written as static HTML. Define the dishes as a data array (each item: name, price, image_url, and optionally a description), loop over it to build the dish cards in the DOM reusing the existing .food-card structure (image + name + price), and replace the hardcoded .food-container cards with an empty container that JavaScript fills in. Clean up the throwaway inline <script> at the bottom of index.html and move this logic into a real file under scripts/ (e.g. scripts/dishes.js) included with <script src=\"...\">. The result should look the same as (or better than) the current hardcoded section — proper cards with images, not a plain bullet list.\n\nAcceptance criteria:\n\n- menu.html, foods.html, about.html exist and contact.html is fully built.\n- Header and footer are defined in one place and identical on every page.\n- All navbar links work and go to the correct page from both the root and pages/.\n- The current active page is visually indicated in the nav.\n- The index.html dishes section is generated from a JS data array (no hardcoded dish cards left in the HTML).\n- Dish rendering lives in a scripts/*.js file, not an inline <script>.\n- No broken links, no console errors, and the layout is consistent across all pages.\n\nNotes: Keep using the existing stylesheets in styles/ — you shouldn't need to rewrite them. Test by clicking through the whole nav in both directions. Keep commits small and descriptive.\n\nRepository to clone from: https://github.com/afanyulionel/restaurant-landing-page.git/",
